course: technology
lesson: 34
title: Batteries
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A battery is a little box of stored chemical energy. Inside, two different chemicals are kept apart; when you join the two ends through a torch or a toy, they react and push electrons all the way round the wire, and the torch lights up.
explain: When the chemicals are used up, the push stops and the battery is flat. A rechargeable battery, like the one in your phone, can be pushed back: the charger moves the tiny particles back to where they started, ready to go again.
explain: Batteries come in every size, from a button in a watch to a car battery that starts the engine, a fridge-sized battery in a house, and grid batteries in rows of shipping containers that help the whole country.
explain: Batteries store a lot of energy in a small space, so they have strong rules: never open, burn or crush one, never mix old and new, keep chargers off beds, and take old ones to the battery bin, never the normal bin.
fact: In a battery such as an AA, two chemicals inside react and push electrons through the wire from one end to the other; when the chemicals are used up, the battery is flat.
fact: Alessandro Volta made the first battery in 1800 from a pile of metal discs and cloth soaked in salty water.
fact: Never open, burn or crush a battery, and never mix old and new batteries in the same toy.
fact: In a rechargeable phone battery, lithium moves from one side to the other as you use it, and charging pushes it back; all such batteries slowly hold less charge as they get older.
fact: Use the right charger, and stop charging if a phone or battery gets hot.
fact: A power bank is a battery in a case that stores electricity to charge phones later; never charge one overnight on a bed, and on planes it goes in hand luggage, not in a checked bag, because if it caught fire down in the hold nobody could reach it to put it out.
fact: A car battery is heavy, starts the engine and powers the lights when the engine is off; it contains acid, so only adults deal with it, and car batteries are among the most recycled products.
fact: Home battery storage keeps electricity from solar panels or cheap night power to use later, and some houses can run for a day on it.
fact: Old batteries go in a special recycling bin so their metals can be used again and their chemicals stay out of landfill; batteries thrown in normal bins have started fires in bin lorries.
fact: Two different metals pushed into a lemon or potato make a tiny current, and several lemons together can light a small LED; this is a science-class activity with low-voltage kits and an adult, never anything to do with mains sockets.
fact: A wireless charger has a coil in the pad that makes a changing magnetic field, and a coil in the phone turns it back into electricity; electric toothbrushes have charged this way for years.
fact: A battery's percentage is an estimate made by a small chip that measures the voltage and how much flows in and out, which is why it sometimes jumps.
fact: Giant grid batteries, made of thousands of cells in containers, store electricity to smooth out wind and solar and can respond in less than a second.
exercise: What makes a battery push electrons round a circuit? | Two chemicals inside reacting | A tiny spinning generator inside | Light stored from the Sun | Air squeezed into the case
exercise: Why does a battery go flat? | The chemicals inside are used up | The electricity leaks out into the air | The case gets too cold | The battery forgets how to work
exercise: Who made the first battery, and when? | Alessandro Volta, in 1800 | Thomas Edison, in 1950 | Isaac Newton, in 1700 | Ada Lovelace, in 1843
exercise: What was the first battery made of? | Metal discs and cloth soaked in salty water | Lemons with copper coins pushed into the skin | Lithium and plastic, like a modern phone battery | Coal and glass jars full of rainwater and sand
exercise: Which rule about batteries is right? | Never mix old and new batteries in one toy | Always mix old and new to use them up | Open a flat battery to see inside | Warm batteries on a radiator to recharge them
exercise: What happens inside a phone battery when you charge it? | The lithium is pushed back to where it started | New lithium flows in through the cable | The battery is filled up with liquid electricity from the plug | The battery is cooled down to store power
exercise: Why does an old phone battery not last as long as a new one? | Batteries hold less charge as they get older | The phone uses a bit more electricity every single year | The charger gets weaker every year and fills it less | A little chip that counts the percentage breaks down
exercise: Where does a power bank go when you fly? | In your hand luggage | In your checked suitcase | In the plane's fuel tank | Anywhere you like
exercise: Who should deal with a car battery? | Only adults, because it contains acid | Any child with gloves on | Anyone, it is just a big AA | Nobody, it never needs anything
exercise: What does home battery storage do? | Stores electricity from solar panels or cheap night power for later | Makes its own electricity from the warm walls and roof of the house all day | Charges the family car by radio while it is out driving on the motorway | Turns the heating and lights off at night so that the house saves money
exercise: Why do old batteries need their own recycling bin? | Their metals can be reused and they can start fires in normal bins | They are too heavy for normal bins | They make the normal bin smell | Bin lorries are only allowed to collect batteries on Sundays and holidays
exercise: Can a lemon really make electricity? | Yes, with two different metals it makes a tiny current | No, fruit can never make electricity, it is only food for us | Yes, enough to run a fridge or a washing machine for a whole day | Only if the lemon is very sour and has been kept in the fridge
exercise: How does a wireless charger charge a phone? | A coil in the pad makes a magnetic field that a coil in the phone turns into electricity | Electricity jumps through the air as sparks | The pad heats the phone until it charges | Light from the pad shines on the screen
exercise: Which everyday thing has charged wirelessly for years? | An electric toothbrush | A gas cooker | A bicycle | A kitchen tap
exercise: Why does a battery percentage sometimes jump, for example from 10 to 0? | It is an estimate made by a chip | The battery suddenly breaks | Someone else is using your phone | The screen shows a random number
exercise: Why does the grid need giant batteries? | To smooth out wind and solar and respond in under a second | To replace all power stations at once | To charge every phone in the country | To keep pylons warm in winter
test: «my AA is dead, just chuck it in the kitchen bin» What is the right answer? | Take it to a battery recycling bin | The kitchen bin is fine for batteries | Burn it in the garden to get rid of it | Bury it in a flower pot
test: «put the new batteries in with the old ones, who cares» What is the right rule? | Never mix old and new batteries | Mixing them makes the toy go faster | Mixing is fine if they are the same colour | Old ones recharge from new ones
test: «phone's getting hot on charge, eh whatever» What should you do? | Stop charging, unplug it and tell an adult | Put it under a pillow so that it cools down quietly | Keep charging so it finishes faster and stops being hot | Put it in the fridge or freezer for an hour to cool it
test: In a cartoon, a hero opens a battery to drink the electricity for power. What is the real rule? | Never open a battery; the chemicals inside are harmful | Batteries are full of a sweet energy drink | Opening it lets the air in and makes the battery last much longer | It is fine if the battery is flat
test: What would happen if a power bank went in a checked suitcase and caught fire in the hold? | No one could reach it to put the fire out, which is why airlines forbid it | Nothing, the hold is fireproof and empty | The suitcase would charge the plane | The pilot would land on the nearest cloud
test: Count them: a remote control needs 2 AA batteries and a torch needs 3. How many for both? | 5 | 6 | 4 | 3
test: Count them: a science class lights an LED with lemons. Each group needs 4 lemons and there are 3 groups. How many lemons? | 12 | 7 | 16 | 4
test: Count them: your phone shows 80% and uses 10% every hour. After 3 hours, about what does it show? | 50% | 70% | 30% | 77%
test: «my brother says a wireless charger squirts electricity through the air like a hose» What is the best correction? | A coil makes a changing magnetic field and a coil in the phone turns it back into electricity | Right, it sprays tiny drops of electricity | It works by heating the phone up | It charges using the light from the room
test: «why does the battery go from like 10 to dead so fast, rubbish phone» What is the real reason? | The percentage is only an estimate made by a chip | The phone hides 10% for itself | Batteries always run down much faster at the very end of the day | The screen counts in tens
test: A house has solar panels. Why might the family want a home battery too? | To use the sunshine's electricity in the evening | To make the panels keep working at night in the dark | To stop the panels getting dirty from rain and bird mess | To sell the battery later for more than they paid for it
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a battery ceases to supply electricity. | The chemical substances within it have been consumed by reaction | The electricity has gradually escaped through the casing into the surrounding air | The external casing has become excessively cold | The terminals have been reversed by the user
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the carriage of a power bank upon an aircraft. | It shall be carried in hand luggage and not in a checked bag | It shall be placed exclusively within a checked suitcase in the hold | It shall be surrendered to the pilot upon boarding the aircraft | No restriction whatsoever applies to its carriage upon an aircraft
exam: Ascertain the reason for which spent batteries are to be deposited in a separate recycling receptacle. | Their metals may be recovered and they may otherwise cause fires in refuse vehicles | They are of excessive weight for ordinary receptacles | They emit an offensive odour in ordinary receptacles | Refuse vehicles are permitted to accept them only upon Sundays and upon public holidays
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of a wireless charger? | A coil in the pad produces a changing magnetic field which a coil in the device converts into electricity | Electricity is transmitted through the air in the form of visible sparks | The device is charged by heat conducted from the pad | The device is charged by light emitted from the pad
